The screen-detection predicates in the following files are ADAPTED from
herdr's TOML screen manifests (`src/detect/manifests/*.toml`):

    Sources/ZeusCore/Resources/manifests/amp.json
    Sources/ZeusCore/Resources/manifests/antigravity.json
    Sources/ZeusCore/Resources/manifests/copilot.json
    Sources/ZeusCore/Resources/manifests/devin.json
    Sources/ZeusCore/Resources/manifests/droid.json
    Sources/ZeusCore/Resources/manifests/grok.json
    Sources/ZeusCore/Resources/manifests/hermes.json
    Sources/ZeusCore/Resources/manifests/kilo.json
    Sources/ZeusCore/Resources/manifests/kimi.json
    Sources/ZeusCore/Resources/manifests/kiro.json
    Sources/ZeusCore/Resources/manifests/opencode.json
    Sources/ZeusCore/Resources/manifests/pi.json
    Sources/ZeusCore/Resources/manifests/qoder.json

Changes made: the rules were translated from herdr's TOML rule schema into
Zeus's JSON predicate schema, re-prioritised for Zeus's evaluator (which
stops at the first matching rule by descending priority), restricted to the
screen regions Zeus implements, and paired with capture regions. Rules that
depend on herdr-only regions (`top_non_empty_lines`, `after_last_horizontal_rule`,
`after_last_prompt_marker`) were dropped rather than approximated.

The `"agent"` capability blocks in those files — binaries, resume specs,
session-id flags, approve/deny keystrokes, env hygiene, injection wiring — are
Zeus's own; herdr does not model most of them. Binary names and resume
argv were cross-checked against herdr's `src/detect/mod.rs` and
`src/agent_resume.rs`.

The manifests for claude-code, codex, cursor, gemini, generic, and shell are
Zeus's own and predate this attribution.
